# Summary
Introduced: the new idle timeout is applied too broadly to long-lived sessions that hold read handles. The timeout bounds idle socket resource use, but it also invalidates the lease semantics of read-handle sessions.
Storage read handles are acquired before reading placed segment shards and released only after the shard reads finish. They prevent shard deletion/reclaim while a read is in progress. This commit applies STORAGE_RPC_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT to every accepted storage-node RPC stream and treats read timeouts as a clean session close. When the timed-out session is dropped, StorageNodeSession releases all acquired read handles. Because a read-handle session normally sends no frames while the protected shard data is being read through separate RPCs, any read taking more than the 1-second idle timeout can lose its deletion fence early. A concurrent delete/reclaim can then proceed against shards that are still being read, or the later explicit release can fail because the server closed the session, causing avoidable GET/read failures. This is primarily an availability/integrity-of-read race rather than an authentication or confidentiality issue.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [1685dc06834c20c9ee3da784fb1c97537698d1a4](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/1685dc06834c20c9ee3da784fb1c97537698d1a4) (`Preserve storage read handles across idle timeout`).

The storage-node RPC idle-timeout path now distinguishes clean disconnects from idle read timeouts. TimedOut/WouldBlock on a session that holds acquired read handles no longer drops the session and releases the read fences, so long shard reads remain protected until the client sends the normal ReadHandlesRelease frame or disconnects.

The preservation is intentionally limited to read-handle-only sessions. If an idle session also holds metadata-command PG locks, timeout cleanup still closes the session so metadata-command locks cannot be kept alive by acquiring an unrelated read handle. Regression coverage now proves both sides of the boundary: active read handles survive idle timeout until explicit release, while mixed read-handle plus metadata-lock sessions are closed and release their locks on timeout.

Validation for the fix included `cargo nextest run -p storage storage_node_server::tests` and `cargo check -p storage`.

