## Report
Validated the finding as a semantic race/lease violation, not a memory-corruption crash. Key code evidence: STORAGE_RPC_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT is 1s in crates/storage/src/storage_rpc.rs:81-82; accepted storage-node RPC sockets get that timeout in crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:1056-1064 and :1276-1284; handle_session treats TimedOut/WouldBlock as a clean session close at crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:1613-1625; StorageNodeSession::drop releases all acquired read handles at crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:10127-10138; deletion is blocked only while handle_counts has an entry at crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:9911-9929 and the delete path calls it at :9096-9105. The real read path acquires read handles, then performs shard reads via separate calls before release in crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:10033-10069, so the read-handle RPC session can be idle during shard I/O. I added a targeted unit PoC using the actual StorageNodeServer and UnixStream RPC path. It acquires a read handle, confirms try_begin_delete returns ResourceExhausted while server.read_handle_count is 1, sleeps STORAGE_RPC_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT + 300ms without sending ReadHandlesRelease, then confirms handle count is 0 and try_begin_delete succeeds while the client still has not explicitly released. Command output: cargo test -p storage poc_idle_timeout_releases_active_read_handle_and_allows_delete -- --nocapture => 1 passed in 1.51s. LLDB trace captured the ordering: first breakpoint at StorageNodeReadHandleState::try_begin_delete from the PoC active-delete check; then breakpoint at StorageNodeSession::drop line 10135 calling shared_handles.release from handle_session/accept_one after timeout; then another try_begin_delete from the PoC after timeout, with the test passing. Direct native crash is not expected for this availability/integrity race. Valgrind was attempted but is unavailable in the container: bash: command not found: valgrind.

