# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Retain medium severity. Static evidence and the executable validation PoC support that the bug is real and in the main product read/delete path: storage-node RPC sessions time out after 1 second, timeouts are treated as clean close, StorageNodeSession::drop releases read handles, and deletion checks only active handle_counts. The issue is reachable from S3 GET/read flows through HttpFrontend and Coordinator, with a concurrent DeleteObject/reclaim side path. However, the impact is limited to availability and integrity of active reads; there is no auth bypass, confidentiality exposure, privilege escalation, or arbitrary code execution. Preconditions are nontrivial: authorized or otherwise permitted S3 access, a read longer than the timeout, and overlapping reclaim/delete pressure. These factors rule out high/critical, but the validated race against storage correctness justifies medium rather than ignore/low.
## Likelihood
high - The vulnerable path is reachable through normal S3 object reads, but exploitation requires a long or slow read exceeding a 1-second internal timeout plus overlapping delete/reclaim timing. The direct RPC surface is a Unix socket, not an exposed network port, and normal S3 authz still applies. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Premature release of read handles can allow shard delete/reclaim while a GET/read is still using the shard. This can cause read failures or integrity-of-read races for affected objects. It does not bypass authentication, expose data, grant code execution, or permit unauthorized deletion; deletion/reclaim must otherwise be authorized or triggered by normal lifecycle/background processing.
## Assumptions
- The normal product deployment is the argmin-s3 runtime, optionally split into frontend and storage-node roles.
- A realistic external attacker is an authenticated S3 client, or an anonymous client only where a bucket is intentionally public.
- The storage-node RPC socket itself is a local Unix socket and is not directly internet-exposed; external reachability is through S3 GET/DELETE and background reclaim flows.
- The race becomes practically observable when shard reads or reconstruction take longer than the 1-second storage RPC idle timeout and deletion/reclaim overlaps with the read.
- S3 frontend process role serving HTTP/HTTPS
- valid SigV4 credentials or object/bucket permissions sufficient for the relevant read/delete operations
- object read path that acquires storage-node read handles
- read or shard I/O lasting longer than STORAGE_RPC_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT
- concurrent delete/reclaim attempt against the same shard/object generation
## Path
S3 client -> HttpFrontend(auth) -> Coordinator ReadHandle -> StorageCluster shard read -> Unix RPC ReadHandlesAcquire -> idle >1s -> session timeout/drop -> read handles released -> concurrent delete/reclaim -> GET/read failure
