## Narrative
The finding is a real in-scope runtime vulnerability at medium severity. The production S3 frontend authenticates requests and dispatches GetObject/DeleteObject operations. The read path creates ReadHandle objects and ultimately reads segment payloads from StorageCluster. The storage cluster acquires per-shard read handles before reading direct shards and only explicitly releases them after shard I/O. However, accepted storage-node RPC streams are configured with a 1-second idle timeout, and handle_session treats TimedOut/WouldBlock as a clean close. Dropping the session releases all acquired read handles. Deletion is blocked only while handle_counts is nonzero, so a timeout can remove the fence before the read completes. The validated executable PoC showed this sequence on the real UnixStream RPC path. Impact is availability/integrity of active reads, not auth bypass, code execution, or confidentiality loss.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication on non-OPTIONS S3 requests
- Coordinator bucket/object authorization before GetObject/DeleteObject
- Default listen address is loopback 127.0.0.1:9000 unless configured otherwise
- Optional direct HTTPS via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
- Connection and in-flight request admission limits
- Storage-node RPC admission limits and wait timeouts
- Process refuses to run as root
- Secret values are wrapped/redacted in Debug implementations
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests, load balancers, container definitions, or Kubernetes service accounts were present, so actual internet exposure and runtime identity cannot be confirmed statically.
- The exact real-world trigger rate depends on object size, shard size, disk latency, erasure-code recovery behavior, and background reclaim scheduling.
- Static review cannot prove all authorization policy combinations that may allow a low-privilege client to combine GET with DELETE/reclaim on shared objects.
- The validation PoC exercised the real Unix RPC path but used a targeted unit scenario rather than a full end-to-end HTTP GET/DeleteObject workload.
